Monthly private-information audit of the ClaudeOS workspace at ${BORG_ROOT}.

This is a public-repository hygiene check, not a credential audit. The daily
`c4po-security-audit` owns API keys, tokens, passwords, `.env` files, secret
files, and credential permissions. Do not duplicate those checks here.

STEP 1: This job is fired by launchd on calendar days 1–5 of each month, but
should only execute once per month. Stop and output nothing if
`${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-privacy-audit-monthly.json`
exists and its `date` field is in the current calendar month.

The scheduler retries on subsequent days if an earlier attempt was missed
(e.g., laptop asleep).

STEP 2: From `${BORG_ROOT}`, audit private identifying information in
git-tracked files only. `git ls-files` is the complete and exclusive file
universe. Do not inspect untracked or ignored files, do not broaden the scan
with `find`, and do not infer that an ignored file is a finding. Read the
tracked working-copy contents of those paths. Skip binary content after
confirming the path came from `git ls-files`.

Use a cheap candidate pass followed by contextual judgment; do not report a
regex match without deciding that it identifies John, another real person, a
real machine, or a real account. Look for:
- real hostnames, machine names, LAN names, and Bonjour (`*.local`) names;
- tailnet IPv4 addresses in `100.64.0.0/10`;
- real usernames and public/private account handles;
- absolute user-home paths such as `/Users/<name>`, `/home/<name>`, and
  `C:\\Users\\<name>`;
- real email addresses and phone numbers;
- account, customer, member, subscriber, policy, portfolio, wallet, or similar
  identifiers when their context connects them to a real person or account.

Examples and obvious templates are not findings. In particular,
`you@gmail.com` in `.env.example` and `selfaware97@gmail.com` in a comment in
`.bin/notify-email.sh` are placeholders, not real addresses. Generic names in
documentation, fixture data that is clearly fictional, commit hashes, dates,
ordinary version numbers, and launchd labels are not account identifiers.

Then perform a model judgment pass for private domain facts. Keep this pass
deliberately narrow: read only tracked `bones/AGENTS.md`, `bones/CLAUDE.md`, and
every tracked file under `warren-bot-fett/`, `norm/`, `ari/`, and `vinny/`.
Decide from meaning and context; do not reduce this class to keywords. Private
medical facts, holdings, balances, tax circumstances, career history,
compensation, application state, agreement facts, and negotiation strategy are
findings. Generic operating instructions, wholly synthetic examples, public
market facts, templates, methodology, and model output with no private
person/account connection are not.

Allowlist — these exact known occurrences are acceptable; the reason is part
of each entry. Do not treat an entire file as exempt, and report any other
identifier in it:
- `mrs-beast/USER.md`: the values `John`, `john-difini`, `JohnDiFini`, and
  `johndifini` (including the listed profile/site URLs) are load-bearing because
  mrs-beast writes public social copy as John.
- `mrs-beast/.claude/scheduled/mrs-beast-social-media-drafts.prompt`: the
  `johndifini` repository-owner occurrences are load-bearing because mrs-beast
  links public social copy and its sources as John.
- `QUICKSTART.md`: the `johndifini` occurrence in the clone URL is load-bearing
  because a public quick-start needs a working clone command.
- `.claude/rules/tracked-agent-files-are-public.md`: the `johndifini/theborg`
  occurrence is load-bearing because naming the public repository is the rule's
  premise.

Control-text exclusion, not a durable allowlist: while the source backlog item
for this audit still exists, do not report the absolute John home path or other
identifiers quoted solely inside that item's own description. They document the
check itself and disappear when the parent session closes the item. This does
not exempt other content in `BACKLOG.md`.

Known limitations: this cannot catch an identifier class nobody thought to
look for, and it cannot catch anything in an untracked or ignored file. Most
genuinely sensitive workspace material is untracked or ignored by design.

Collect each finding as `{file, line, class, matched_text, context,
suggested_remediation}`. Remediation guidance must use the workspace's existing
privacy conventions: move machine-specific instructions into a directory's
gitignored `.claude/rules/*.local.md` or `.claude/settings.local.json`; keep
secrets/local configuration in ignored `.env*`; keep confidential agent data
under that agent's ignored `.private/` directory; and publish only synthetic
templates under `.private.example/`. Do not suggest
parameterizing tracked text with `${BORG_ROOT}`, `$USER`, or similar variables.

Every remediation must carry a cross-reference check. After removing an
identifier from a tracked file, re-check every reference that pointed at it —
a section number, form name, defined term, or "see above" that resolved only
via the removed text. Redaction routinely leaves a dangling pointer, and the
result is confidently wrong rather than obviously broken: the sentence still
reads, the citation still looks authoritative, and it now names nothing.
Observed 2026-08-16, when genericizing a state name left a tracked prompt
citing form numbers that existed nowhere in the private file it cited.

Two scanning failures to design around, both observed the same day on the same
file. A scan for `employer` missed a leak because the file named the companies
inline and never used the word; a substring scan for a company name then hit
`edgecolor` in unrelated plotting code. Denylists fail in BOTH directions, so
do not try to fix a miss by lengthening the pattern list. Read a file's stated
privacy rule and check whether that rule enumerates categories narrowly enough
to be obeyed while still leaking — the 2026-08-16 file forbade "private
financial figures" and so complied with itself while naming two employers.
A rule naming one category invites a reviewer to scan for exactly that
category and stop; report that framing as the finding, not just the string.

STEP 3: If there is at least one finding, pipe a Markdown report into
`${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg privacy audit — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {total} finding(s)"`
(reads from stdin), then STOP. Touch no files, open no backlog item, and attempt
no remediation. John decides whether each identifier is acceptable. Format:
- Header: `Borg privacy audit — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {total} finding(s)`
- One bullet per finding: `<file>:<line> — <class>: <matched_text> — <context>
  → <suggested_remediation>`
- End with the two known limitations from STEP 2.

A finding may mention that the same string also appears in git history if that
context is useful, but history is outside remediation scope. Never propose or
perform a git history rewrite.

STEP 4: If there are no findings, pipe this Markdown body into
`${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg privacy audit — {YYYY-MM-DD} — 0 finding(s)"`:

`All clear — no findings. The two documented mrs-beast identity occurrences are allowlisted.`

STEP 5: After the clean report only, create the state directory if needed and
write `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-privacy-audit-monthly.json`
with `{"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "findings": 0}` so the run is not repeated this
month. A findings run must never reach this step.
